
# Assignment 3.2 — Images in HTML

## 1. Objective
To add an images section to a portfolio webpage using the HTML `<img>` tag, correct image paths, meaningful `alt` text, and appropriate image dimensions.

## 2. Project Folder Structure

Create a project folder named `portfolio` with the following structure:

portfolio/
│
├── index.html
└── images/
    ├── profile.jpg
    ├── coding.jpg
    └── project.jpg

Place at least three image files inside the `images` folder. You can use your own images and rename them to match the filenames above.

## 3. HTML Code (index.html)

Create an `index.html` file and add the following code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Portfolio - Images</title>
</head>
<body>

    <header>
        <h1>My Portfolio</h1>
        <p>Welcome to my personal portfolio website.</p>
    </header>

    <hr>

    <section id="about">
        <h2>About Me</h2>
        <p>Hello! I am a web development student learning HTML and CSS.</p>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <section id="images">
        <h2>My Images</h2>

        <div>
            <h3>My Profile</h3>
            <img
                src="images/profile.jpg"
                alt="My personal profile photograph"
                width="300"
                height="200">
        </div>

        <div>
            <h3>Coding Practice</h3>
            <img
                src="images/coding.jpg"
                alt="A computer screen displaying programming code"
                width="300"
                height="200">
        </div>

        <div>
            <h3>My Project</h3>
            <img
                src="images/project.jpg"
                alt="A screenshot or photograph of my web development project"
                width="300"
                height="200">
        </div>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <footer>
        <p>&copy; 2026 My Portfolio. All rights reserved.</p>
    </footer>

</body>
</html>
```

## 4. Explanation

- `src`: Specifies the location of the image file.
- `alt`: Provides meaningful alternative text if the image cannot load and helps screen-reader users understand the image.
- `width`: Sets the displayed image width.
- `height`: Sets the displayed image height.
- `images/`: The folder containing the three image files.
- `<section>`: Organizes the images into a separate section of the portfolio.

## 5. How to Run and Test

1. Create the `portfolio` folder.
2. Create an `images` folder inside it.
3. Add three images and name them `profile.jpg`, `coding.jpg`, and `project.jpg`.
4. Save the HTML code as `index.html` in the `portfolio` folder.
5. Open `index.html` in your browser, or use Live Server in VS Code.
6. Check that all three images load correctly.
7. If an image does not appear, verify its filename, file extension, and path.

Important: The filenames and extensions in your HTML must match your actual image files. If your image is named `profile.png`, change the path to `images/profile.png`.

## 6. Expected Result

Your portfolio webpage should display:

- A heading titled My Portfolio.
- An About Me section.
- A My Images section containing three images.
- A meaningful heading and alternative text for each image.
- A footer at the bottom of the page.

